A1. These are always bringing up the dirt. ~ ANTS
A5. We hope you get, at least, this out of JUDGE. ~ SMILE
A10. What do little boys usually want at dinner? ~ MORE
A14. A sore point. ~ BOIL
A15. What the horses in the old county fair did to win the race. ~ PACED
A16. The shape of an ellipse. ~ OVAL
A17. To catch sight of. ~ ESPY
A18. These are hard to catch, but when caught should be put to work. ~ IDEAS
A19. Beastly hang-outs. ~ DENS
A20. Where the speak-easy number should be popular. ~ DESERT
A22. What debs do at a coming out party. ~ EMERGE
A24. What Kearns did to Dempsey. ~ SUED
A26. The pessimist's pet word. ~ ALAS
A27. This has a lot of sticktoitiveness. ~ PASTE
A30. An attachment needed with every radio. ~ EAR
A32. A sucker. ~ STRAW
A36. How many bootleggers believe in prohibition? ~ ALL
A37. What'does a toreador talk with his senorita? ~ SPANISH
A40. The origin of the iron-cross. ~ ORE
A41. Many a peach has gone into this. ~ PIE
A42. Appearing as if gnawed. ~ EROSE
A43. To employ. ~ USE
A44. A spare rib. ~ EVE
A45. A good thing for a business man to have. ~ CREDITS
A47. The elephant of the cartoonists. ~ GOP
A48. To cause aversion. ~ REPEL
A50. A Japanese coin. ~ SEN
A51. Curved figures—and under twenty. ~ TEENS
A53. You can raise this on any old car. ~ DUST
A55. A good thing to put your car in. ~ GEAR
A57. What the Irish do to the Scotch. ~ IMBIBE
A60. These are always left behind. ~ TRACKS
A64. This made the Leaning Tower of Pisa famous. ~ TILT
A65. Often met up with in Chicago. ~ DEATH
A69. The kind of person who never marries more than once. ~ SANE
A70. What the ambitious sleuth follows. ~ CLUE
A71. What the young and old never do. ~ AGREE
A72. There is no end to this. ~ EVER
A73. To take notice. ~ HEED
A74. We suppose you could call a stenographer this. ~ NOTER
A75. A navy man should not get stuck on this. ~ REEF

D1. Where retired millionaires are. ~ ABED
D2. This is good for a scent. ~ NOSE
D3. Servant's quarters. ~ TIPS
D4. The most artful. ~ SLYEST
D5. What causes women to say catty things to each other? ~ SPITE
D6. What you'd be if they put you in Matteawan. ~ MAD
D7. Hard drink. ~ ICE
D8. The poetic landscape. ~ LEA
D9. Lady Lizzie's brother. ~ EDSEL
D10. The kind of clothes grandma used to wear. ~ MODEST
D11. Which way do some ears turn when approaching a ditch? ~ OVER
D12. What she did to get service. ~ RANG
D13. Otherwise. ~ ELSE
D21. What the Scotchman does when he loses. ~ RUES
D23. A kind of note which often causes trouble. ~ MASH
D25. A popular form of address among men of letters. ~ DEAREST
D26. A person must get busy about this if he wants to get up in the world. ~ ARISING
D27. This is often overlooked at breakfast time. ~ PAPER
D28. No dead one can be in this condition. ~ ALIVE
D29. An all night affair. ~ SLEEP
D31. The positive terminal of an electrical source. ~ ANODE
D33. This should be taken at its face value. ~ ROUGE
D34. What it is—sometimes—to monkey with fire. ~ ARSON
D35. What many a girl does to win an argument. ~ WEEPS
D38. By means of. ~ PER
D39. What any goose would do for her young. ~ SET
D45. What won in pre-historic poker. ~ CLUB
D46. A heavenly body—on Broadway. ~ STAR
D49. Prepared for publication. ~ EDITED
D52. An aid in solving crossword puzzles. ~ ERASER
D54. The covered wagon. ~ SEDAN
D56. Beyond the sky limit. ~ ETHER
D57. A ticklish situation. ~ ITCH
D58. They say you can get over this in a minute in the new Ford. ~ MILE
D59. A popular color for losers. ~ BLUE
D61. Where the first clubman lived. ~ CAVE
D62. It's hard for the girls to keep this under cover. ~ KNEE
D63. A slave bound to the soil. ~ SERF
D66. Me and my shadow. ~ EGO
D67. Skill. ~ ART
D68. A ball-bearing starter. ~ TEE


